1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
It takes water to make cities grow.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:06,740
And without water, cities would die.

3
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:08,740
New York, Chicago, and Los Angeles

4
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:12,440
need billions of gallons a day, from rivers and reservoirs

5
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:15,440
to the water tap. A vast hidden network

6
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:17,960
of tunnels, pipes, and pumps.

7
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:21,660
Some are cutting edge, and some are a century old.

8
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:25,660
Now, City Water on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
October 2003, a 108-year-old New York City water main carrying

10
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:48,000
more than a million gallons an hour suddenly burst, and swirling

11
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:51,000
whitewater rapids surged through an area of several square

12
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:53,000
blocks. The disruption,

13
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:56,000
dislocation, and damage were dramatic.

14
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:59,500
The incident pointed out how fragile and yet indispensable

15
00:00:59,501 --> 00:01:04,000
city water systems are. Most of us take them for granted

16
00:01:04,001 --> 00:01:08,000
until there's a problem. When most Americans turn on a

17
00:01:08,001 --> 00:01:12,000
faucet, they expect clean, pure water to flow out. They also

18
00:01:12,001 --> 00:01:15,000
expect their city to have plenty of water for industries, for

19
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:19,000
fighting fires, and for cleaning streets.

20
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:22,000
Public water supply systems in the United States provide all

21
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:26,340
provide all those things for 99% of the population, over a

22
00:01:26,341 --> 00:01:29,240
quarter of a billion people.

23
00:01:29,241 --> 00:01:31,940
Water supply is empowering.

24
00:01:31,941 --> 00:01:35,040
There are so many things that can happen when you have a water

25
00:01:35,041 --> 00:01:38,460
supply, and so many things that don't happen when you don't

26
00:01:38,461 --> 00:01:41,160
have a water supply.

27
00:01:41,161 --> 00:01:43,960
On average, Americans use about 100 gallons of water

28
00:01:43,961 --> 00:01:46,260
per person every day.

29
00:01:46,261 --> 00:01:49,400
And public waterworks in the U.S. supply about 40 billion

30
00:01:49,401 --> 00:01:51,400
gallons daily.

31
00:01:51,401 --> 00:01:53,260
It truly is an invisible empire.

32
00:01:53,261 --> 00:01:57,620
There are thousands of miles of these aqueducts and tunnels

33
00:01:57,621 --> 00:02:00,120
all underneath the city, crisscrossing and moving.

34
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,640
There's very little space down there they've built so much.

35
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:04,620
And these guys have really built,

36
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:06,980
like the men who built the skyscrapers up,

37
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:08,900
they've built the skyscrapers down.

38
00:02:10,040 --> 00:02:13,180
Some of the technology used in today's city water systems

39
00:02:13,181 --> 00:02:14,500
has been around for millennia.

40
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,560
Sanskrit writings from 4,000 years ago

41
00:02:18,561 --> 00:02:21,340
talk about the importance of purifying drinking water.

42
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:25,340
and describe various methods, including boiling over a fire

43
00:02:25,341 --> 00:02:27,480
and filtering through sand or charcoal.

44
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:32,960
2,700 years ago, kanats, sloping tunnels carrying groundwater,

45
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:35,100
were being used in northwestern Persia.

46
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:38,880
The kanats are basically horizontal wells

47
00:02:38,881 --> 00:02:40,440
that are used for irrigation.

48
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:44,320
These are developed originally in the area,

49
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,120
mainly what's today Iran.

50
00:02:46,820 --> 00:02:50,900
They spread throughout the area into areas like Spain,

51
00:02:50,901 --> 00:02:56,280
into parts of China. Around 312 B.C., four

52
00:02:56,281 --> 00:02:59,640
centuries after Canats first appeared in Persia, construction

53
00:02:59,641 --> 00:03:03,740
on the renowned aqueduct system of ancient Rome began.

54
00:03:03,741 --> 00:03:06,940
An extraordinary feat of engineering, they brought water

55
00:03:06,941 --> 00:03:10,860
to the city from as far as 57 miles away.

56
00:03:10,861 --> 00:03:15,060
Over a period of 500 years, 11 aqueducts were built to bring

57
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:20,900
water to Rome, and some sections are still functioning today.

58
00:03:20,901 --> 00:03:24,400
Ancient Mayans in the Yucatan Peninsula got their fresh water

59
00:03:24,401 --> 00:03:27,400
from naturally formed underground sinkholes called

60
00:03:27,401 --> 00:03:31,400
cenotes, to which they attached great religious significance.

61
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:34,900
The Maya considered the cenotes portals to the underworld,

62
00:03:34,901 --> 00:03:37,400
where they would journey in the afterlife to meet gods and

63
00:03:37,401 --> 00:03:39,900
ancestors. Probably the most well-known

64
00:03:39,901 --> 00:03:43,400
one is the cenote at the big site of Chichen Itza in Yucatan

65
00:03:43,401 --> 00:03:46,400
State. And this has yielded a tremendous

66
00:03:46,401 --> 00:03:50,400
amount of materials, offerings that were tossed in over the

67
00:03:50,401 --> 00:03:54,840
over the years. Fancy pottery, very late in

68
00:03:54,841 --> 00:04:01,080
the history, gold, and a few humans for good measure.

69
00:04:01,081 --> 00:04:04,540
In larger Mayan settlements south of the area where

70
00:04:04,541 --> 00:04:08,220
cenotes are found, huge reservoirs were constructed to

71
00:04:08,221 --> 00:04:11,580
capture runoff water. At Tikal, which is one of

72
00:04:11,581 --> 00:04:16,000
the larger Maya sites in the southern lowlands, probably held

73
00:04:16,001 --> 00:04:18,920
about at least 50,000 people in that city.

74
00:04:18,921 --> 00:04:23,160
They had, just in the central precinct of Tikal alone, the

75
00:04:23,161 --> 00:04:27,620
reservoirs could hold something in the area of about 200,000

76
00:04:27,621 --> 00:04:31,740
cubic meters of water, quite a bit.

77
00:04:31,741 --> 00:04:34,240
Residents of medieval London might have been envious of the

78
00:04:34,241 --> 00:04:38,540
Mayan reservoirs. At the beginning of the 13th century,

79
00:04:38,541 --> 00:04:40,980
the increase in London's population was creating a

80
00:04:40,981 --> 00:04:44,320
growing pollution problem in the existing water supplies, which

81
00:04:44,321 --> 00:04:47,580
came from local wells and the Thames River.

82
00:04:47,581 --> 00:04:51,420
Across all of London, there were then these cesspits,

83
00:04:51,421 --> 00:04:54,240
and there were, of course, these wells, sometimes right next

84
00:04:54,241 --> 00:04:58,440
to each other in ways that were just extraordinary.

85
00:04:58,441 --> 00:05:01,220
In 1236, construction was authorized

86
00:05:01,221 --> 00:05:03,820
on the Great Conduit, which would bring drinking water

87
00:05:03,821 --> 00:05:08,540
to the city from a spring at Tyburn, three miles away.

88
00:05:08,541 --> 00:05:11,840
Eventually, several conduit houses were built in London.

89
00:05:11,841 --> 00:05:13,980
The stone buildings were not only used for storing

90
00:05:13,981 --> 00:05:15,620
and dispensing water.

91
00:05:15,621 --> 00:05:17,420
they also served as social centers.

92
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:21,420
This was a place where people gathered during festival times.

93
00:05:21,421 --> 00:05:24,200
They decorated the side with garlands.

94
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:26,200
They painted slogans on it.

95
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:29,000
And on real special occasions, like, for example,

96
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:33,280
the coronation of Anne Boleyn in 1532,

97
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:36,240
they stopped the flow of water and put wine in

98
00:05:36,241 --> 00:05:39,640
so that everybody could have wine delivered to them.

99
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:44,480
Still, many Londoners continued to get their drinking water

100
00:05:44,481 --> 00:05:48,800
from the Thames River, despite the pollution from human and animal waste dumped in the river.

101
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:54,060
The resulting illnesses weren't associated with the pollution because of lack of knowledge of the

102
00:05:54,061 --> 00:05:59,740
microorganisms that cause disease. Sometimes the night air was blamed for

103
00:05:59,741 --> 00:06:01,732
certain types of death that would come.

104
00:06:01,733 --> 00:06:04,440
Every summer it was almost regular, however, that the

105
00:06:04,441 --> 00:06:08,740
so-called sweating sickness would come in, which is probably a type of typhoid

106
00:06:08,741 --> 00:06:14,020
that we know now, but they didn't know it. Like the residents of medieval London,

107
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:18,480
early New Yorkers were completely unaware that their drinking water was making them sick,

108
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:24,080
and thousands would die of waterborne diseases, like cholera, before they made the connection.

109
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:29,060
The attempt to bring fresh water to New York City would lead to some of the great water projects

110
00:06:29,061 --> 00:06:30,288
of the 20th century.

111
00:06:30,289 --> 00:06:34,340
Manhattan's fresh water initially came from shallow private wells,

112
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:40,420
until the first public well was dug in 1677 in front of the old fort at Bowling Green.

113
00:06:40,421 --> 00:06:44,860
By the late 1770s, Manhattan's 22,000 residents

114
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:46,860
were getting most of their drinking water

115
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:48,760
from the highly polluted Collect Pond

116
00:06:48,860 --> 00:06:50,360
in the heart of the city.

117
00:06:50,460 --> 00:06:52,860
Because people were bathing in the Collect,

118
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:54,360
it quickly became dirty.

119
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:56,360
It became incredibly fetid.

120
00:06:56,460 --> 00:06:59,180
There was a dead body, which occasionally washed up in it

121
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:00,680
from a crime scene.

122
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:04,480
People would find dead animals, feces.

123
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:07,580
The contaminated water led to a disastrous cholera

124
00:07:07,581 --> 00:07:09,980
cholera epidemic in 1832.

125
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:12,280
It went from hundreds and then into the thousands

126
00:07:12,380 --> 00:07:14,920
of people who died from that epidemic.

127
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:17,520
Kids would plea for water, and then the water

128
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:19,620
that they would give them was the very thing that was making

129
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:21,720
them sick and only made the epidemic worse.

130
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:23,820
The situation deteriorated even further

131
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:27,240
in 1835 when a small fire broke out in a single building

132
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:28,840
on Wall Street.

133
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:30,340
It was a cold winter night.

134
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:33,580
Wells were frozen over, and so were the rivers.

135
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:36,840
And within 24 hours, all of Lower Manhattan

136
00:07:36,841 --> 00:07:38,680
was ablaze and in ruins.

137
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:42,240
Millions of dollars equivalent were lost from business

138
00:07:42,340 --> 00:07:44,580
and industry, one of the worst disasters

139
00:07:44,680 --> 00:07:46,580
in the history of the United States.

140
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:49,780
And it was these events, these really cataclysmic events,

141
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:56,060
that finally set New York on a concerted program to say,

142
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:57,260
we need to solve this crisis.

143
00:07:57,360 --> 00:07:59,020
How can we do it?

144
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:03,200
In 1837, construction began on the Croton Aqueduct,

145
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:06,240
which still carries water to New York City today, providing

146
00:08:06,241 --> 00:08:09,240
about 10% of the city's water supply.

147
00:08:09,241 --> 00:08:12,540
The 30-mile-long brick pipeline, extending from the Croton

148
00:08:12,541 --> 00:08:15,680
Reservoir along the east bank of the Hudson River to downtown New

149
00:08:15,681 --> 00:08:20,080
York City, took five years to complete, with up to 4,000

150
00:08:20,081 --> 00:08:23,400
laborers on the job at one time.

151
00:08:23,401 --> 00:08:27,300
In 1842, when the aqueduct finally went into service,

152
00:08:27,301 --> 00:08:30,600
President Tyler and former presidents John Quincy Adams

153
00:08:30,601 --> 00:08:33,600
and Martin Van Buren came to the Big Apple for the opening

154
00:08:33,601 --> 00:08:37,140
ceremony and watched water gush for the first time from the

155
00:08:37,141 --> 00:08:40,740
Croton Fountain near City Hall.

156
00:08:40,741 --> 00:08:44,240
People recounted in elaborate fashion their first bath,

157
00:08:44,241 --> 00:08:47,140
their first shower. Things hard to imagine what it

158
00:08:47,141 --> 00:08:49,780
was like to be able to go into your house, turn on a tap, and

159
00:08:49,781 --> 00:08:52,480
have water. The Croton

160
00:08:52,481 --> 00:08:55,820
Aqueduct was considered an architectural marvel.

161
00:08:55,821 --> 00:09:00,400
Most of it was an 8-by-7-foot arched tube of brick and stone

162
00:09:00,401 --> 00:09:03,040
buried 15 to 30 feet beneath the ground.

163
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:04,540
But there were elevated sections,

164
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:07,000
like the High Bridge, an arched brick overpass

165
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:10,240
that took the aqueduct across the Harlem River to Manhattan.

166
00:09:10,340 --> 00:09:12,040
It became a popular tourist attraction

167
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:15,540
in the late 1800s, with sightseers traveling eight miles

168
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:20,960
from the city to stroll across the 1,400-foot long span.

169
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:24,660
Then, as always happens, because of the supply of water,

170
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:26,460
because of the nature of New York City,

171
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,860
the city quickly expanded. And already within 12 years,

172
00:09:29,861 --> 00:09:31,900
It was an editorial in the New York Times saying,

173
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:32,940
we need more water.

174
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:34,080
We need more water.

175
00:09:34,580 --> 00:09:37,160
When you think of the immigration that was occurring

176
00:09:37,161 --> 00:09:41,380
in the end of the 1800s, New York was just exploding,

177
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:45,640
and that Croton network just wasn't able to meet water supply demands.

178
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:49,040
And this time, they looked even further afield,

179
00:09:49,041 --> 00:09:52,920
and this time, their plan and vision was even bolder.

180
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:56,400
And what they did is they decided to build an aqueduct

181
00:09:56,401 --> 00:09:58,360
all the way out to the Catskill Mountains.

182
00:09:58,361 --> 00:10:01,000
The new aqueduct from the Catskill Mountains

183
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:04,500
would be 92 miles long, three times as long

184
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:06,000
as the Croton Aqueduct.

185
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:08,800
It would run down alongside the Hudson River

186
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:11,100
and at one point cross the river.

187
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:14,000
This is another hand-built aqueduct.

188
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:17,780
This was the working man down there with sticks of dynamite

189
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:22,020
and steam locomotives and mules hauling all that rock out.

190
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:26,460
With the Croton Aqueduct, they decided to cross the river

191
00:10:26,461 --> 00:10:29,860
the river and the aqueduct above the river.

192
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:33,960
With the Catskill Aqueduct, they decided to go beneath it.

193
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:36,800
And for over a year, they would sit on these boats pitched

194
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:39,200
in the river, often in storms and rain,

195
00:10:39,300 --> 00:10:42,380
trying to do soundings, trying to literally lower things down

196
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:45,020
to figure out where the bottom of the river was

197
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:47,680
so they could decide how far they had to dig down on the banks

198
00:10:47,780 --> 00:10:49,280
to get underneath it.

199
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:52,280
And there was at one point, people and engineers

200
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:53,620
raised their hands in frustration.

201
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:55,260
And they said, this river is bottomless.

202
00:10:55,261 --> 00:10:57,900
But eventually, the city's engineers did find the bottom

203
00:10:57,901 --> 00:11:00,840
of the river and made the daunting discovery that they

204
00:11:00,841 --> 00:11:04,560
would have to tunnel at a depth of 1,200 feet to get to the

205
00:11:04,561 --> 00:11:07,280
solid and stable bedrock beneath the river.

206
00:11:07,281 --> 00:11:10,440
It was a task that seemed impossible, but they would have

207
00:11:10,441 --> 00:11:11,940
to find a way.

208
00:11:11,941 --> 00:11:14,780
What they didn't know is that the system they were building

209
00:11:14,781 --> 00:11:18,720
had an Achilles heel, a potentially fatal flaw that has

210
00:11:18,721 --> 00:11:23,660
New York City engineers worried today.

211
00:11:23,661 --> 00:11:26,720
only 1% of the fresh water consumed daily in New York City

212
00:11:26,721 --> 00:11:28,380
is used for drinking water.

213
00:11:38,020 --> 00:11:41,600
We now return to City Water on Modern Marvels.

214
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:46,640
When New York City water engineers set out in 1907

215
00:11:46,641 --> 00:11:49,100
to build a new aqueduct from the Catskill Mountains,

216
00:11:49,760 --> 00:11:51,200
one of the most difficult challenges

217
00:11:51,201 --> 00:11:53,640
was digging a tunnel deeper than they had ever

218
00:11:53,641 --> 00:11:55,300
dug before.

219
00:11:55,301 --> 00:11:57,680
In order to cross underneath the Hudson River,

220
00:11:57,681 --> 00:12:00,740
they would have to tunnel at a depth of 1,200 feet.

221
00:12:00,741 --> 00:12:03,480
1,200 feet is nearly as deep underground

222
00:12:03,481 --> 00:12:05,080
as the Empire State Building is high.

223
00:12:05,081 --> 00:12:06,860
So it gives you some sense of scale

224
00:12:06,861 --> 00:12:10,920
of how enormous the engineering was to do this.

225
00:12:10,921 --> 00:12:13,800
And so what they did is they started to dig from both banks,

226
00:12:13,801 --> 00:12:15,320
literally from the sides.

227
00:12:15,321 --> 00:12:17,940
And they began to dig like an inverted triangle coming down

228
00:12:17,941 --> 00:12:23,160
like this until the two sides literally met 1,200 feet

229
00:12:23,161 --> 00:12:27,600
the river, almost like a pincer. After crossing under the Hudson

230
00:12:27,601 --> 00:12:30,600
River, the new aqueduct would flow into City Tunnel Number

231
00:12:30,601 --> 00:12:34,600
One, a huge new tunnel that would run the length of Manhattan,

232
00:12:34,601 --> 00:12:38,720
900 feet deep. Work on the 18-mile-long Tunnel

233
00:12:38,721 --> 00:12:43,220
Number One began in 1911 and went on for six years.

234
00:12:43,221 --> 00:12:46,120
When completed, it would be the longest tunnel of its kind in

235
00:12:46,121 --> 00:12:49,460
the world, capable of carrying 500 million gallons of water

236
00:12:49,461 --> 00:12:53,460
The workers of Tunnel One were members of a proud profession

237
00:12:53,560 --> 00:12:57,460
that continues to build the New York City water system today.

238
00:12:57,560 --> 00:13:00,700
Known as sand hogs, they've been laboring underground,

239
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:04,100
unknown to most New Yorkers, for decades.

240
00:13:04,200 --> 00:13:06,620
They got the name from their work in the sandy bottom

241
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:08,980
of the East River, laying foundations

242
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:11,120
for the Brooklyn Bridge.

243
00:13:11,220 --> 00:13:14,480
Starting at the absolute bottom of the economic ladder,

244
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:18,520
the hardest, toughest, in a sense, most dangerous

245
00:13:18,521 --> 00:13:21,160
dangerous job out there that you could find.

246
00:13:21,260 --> 00:13:25,160
Fathers passed on those job opportunities to their children,

247
00:13:25,260 --> 00:13:28,160
and a culture grew out of that.

248
00:13:28,260 --> 00:13:31,460
Construction of the 11 to 15-foot diameter tunnel

249
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:33,760
was particularly dangerous where it crossed the East

250
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:36,380
River to carry water to Brooklyn.

251
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:39,140
Natural groundwater made the ground so soft

252
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:41,780
that the shafts in which workers descended to the tunnel

253
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:43,980
became watery death traps.

254
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:45,720
Every time they would dig, the soil

255
00:13:45,820 --> 00:13:47,320
would cave in on top of them.

256
00:13:47,321 --> 00:13:49,020
So what they decided they needed to do

257
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:51,060
was to sink something called a caisson.

258
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:54,060
Pneumatic caissons filled with pressurized air

259
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:57,200
had been used earlier in the building of the Brooklyn Bridge.

260
00:13:57,300 --> 00:13:59,160
These concrete and steel structures weighed

261
00:13:59,260 --> 00:14:03,000
about 2,000 tons each.

262
00:14:03,100 --> 00:14:05,700
They were sealed on all sides except the bottom.

263
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:07,940
And as they were lowered into the soft ground,

264
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:12,280
compressed air was pumped in, pushing out the mud and water.

265
00:14:12,380 --> 00:14:15,720
That pumping of the air created a real safety health

266
00:14:15,721 --> 00:14:18,360
because it created an environment where the workers

267
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:19,720
would get the bends.

268
00:14:19,820 --> 00:14:23,760
So they were only allowed to be down there for two hours.

269
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:26,600
The bends, also known as caisson disease,

270
00:14:26,700 --> 00:14:29,840
can happen when pressure on the body is removed too quickly,

271
00:14:29,940 --> 00:14:32,240
causing the formation of nitrogen bubbles in the blood

272
00:14:32,340 --> 00:14:34,180
and tissues.

273
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:35,680
Sometimes the intense air pressure

274
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:39,840
would cause a dangerous blowout in the walls of the caisson.

275
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:44,720
It was basically a high, shrill, kettle-like screeching,

276
00:14:44,721 --> 00:14:46,520
which would echo in the caisson.

277
00:14:46,620 --> 00:14:48,920
And basically, it meant there was a rupture in the lining

278
00:14:49,020 --> 00:14:51,760
of the caisson wall, that the air was now escaping,

279
00:14:51,860 --> 00:14:53,620
created an incredible suction.

280
00:14:53,720 --> 00:14:55,760
And everything in the tunnel was going to be sucked

281
00:14:55,860 --> 00:14:57,360
towards a small opening.

282
00:14:57,460 --> 00:14:59,200
And because of the pressure, the opening could widen

283
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:00,340
very quickly.

284
00:15:00,440 --> 00:15:03,600
And everyone would drop their picks and run.

285
00:15:03,700 --> 00:15:05,880
Most of the concrete line tunnel number one

286
00:15:05,980 --> 00:15:08,280
had to be blasted through solid rock.

287
00:15:08,380 --> 00:15:10,480
During the first three years of construction,

288
00:15:10,580 --> 00:15:13,880
10,000 pounds of dynamite were used daily.

289
00:15:13,881 --> 00:15:18,620
By the end of 1912, a total of 2.5 million pounds of dynamite

290
00:15:18,621 --> 00:15:23,120
had been used, and 14 workers had died in blasting accidents.

291
00:15:23,121 --> 00:15:27,120
The tunnel was finally finished in 1917, and a lavish opening

292
00:15:27,121 --> 00:15:30,120
ceremony was planned at a fountain in Central Park.

293
00:15:30,121 --> 00:15:34,240
Entitled The Good Gift of Water, the pageant had to be cancelled

294
00:15:34,241 --> 00:15:37,640
because of rain. Tunnel number one was an

295
00:15:37,641 --> 00:15:40,940
engineering triumph, but for a city growing as fast as New

296
00:15:40,941 --> 00:15:44,932
New York. It would be inadequate within a few years.

297
00:15:44,933 --> 00:15:48,080
Over the next decade, population expanded

298
00:15:48,081 --> 00:15:54,520
eastward from the Bronx and Manhattan, and by 1929, water consumption was 35 million gallons a day

299
00:15:54,521 --> 00:15:59,000
higher. Construction began on Tunnel Number 2 to supply Brooklyn and Queens.

300
00:15:59,001 --> 00:16:00,120
The tunnel would be

301
00:16:00,121 --> 00:16:03,436
fed by a new aqueduct from the Catskill region.

302
00:16:03,437 --> 00:16:06,540
It connects with City Tunnel Number 2, which

303
00:16:06,541 --> 00:16:09,340
which, together with City Tunnel 1,

304
00:16:09,341 --> 00:16:12,140
delivers all of New York's water

305
00:16:12,141 --> 00:16:15,420
to 8 million people in New York City.

306
00:16:15,421 --> 00:16:18,780
Obviously, they are of crucial importance.

307
00:16:18,781 --> 00:16:23,500
Without one or the other, half the city would be without water.

308
00:16:23,501 --> 00:16:26,500
In the 1950s, New York City water engineers

309
00:16:26,501 --> 00:16:29,660
made a disturbing discovery when they tried to shut down

310
00:16:29,661 --> 00:16:32,540
a section of City Tunnel Number 1 to inspect it.

311
00:16:32,541 --> 00:16:35,940
They had built valves in the bottom of shafts,

312
00:16:35,941 --> 00:16:37,440
They would literally turn them.

313
00:16:37,540 --> 00:16:38,540
They would turn.

314
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:40,320
They almost look like a steering wheel on a submarine,

315
00:16:40,420 --> 00:16:41,820
sticking out of a hatch.

316
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:44,680
So in 1954, the city goes down.

317
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:46,020
The engineers go down.

318
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:47,960
They start to turn the handle.

319
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:49,580
And as they start to turn the handle,

320
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:52,520
it begins to tremble, begins to shake.

321
00:16:52,620 --> 00:16:54,360
There's too much pressure on it.

322
00:16:54,460 --> 00:16:55,400
It's too old.

323
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:56,620
The valve's corroded.

324
00:16:56,720 --> 00:16:58,260
They can't shut it off.

325
00:16:58,360 --> 00:16:59,800
They couldn't repair it.

326
00:16:59,900 --> 00:17:01,900
They couldn't even find out if it was about to burst

327
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:03,460
or about to break.

328
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:05,940
It scared the bejesus, as one engineer said.

329
00:17:05,941 --> 00:17:08,840
told me at the time, out of everybody.

330
00:17:08,841 --> 00:17:10,680
If you lose this system, something

331
00:17:10,681 --> 00:17:13,580
that New Yorkers take for granted day in and day out,

332
00:17:13,581 --> 00:17:16,180
you'd have a very different New York City without water.

333
00:17:16,181 --> 00:17:18,620
And that is, one, a daunting challenge,

334
00:17:18,621 --> 00:17:22,520
and one that makes the third water tunnel so critical.

335
00:17:22,521 --> 00:17:25,020
City tunnel number one has been in continuous use

336
00:17:25,021 --> 00:17:27,760
without a shutdown for inspection or maintenance

337
00:17:27,761 --> 00:17:30,340
for well over 100 years.

338
00:17:30,341 --> 00:17:33,000
Hundreds of sand hogs are now fighting a crucial battle

339
00:17:33,001 --> 00:17:35,320
to complete a new tunnel as quickly as possible.

340
00:17:37,100 --> 00:17:40,020
There's no way to bring water to lower Manhattan

341
00:17:40,021 --> 00:17:43,400
from 59th Street down without tunnel number one.

342
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:46,320
Once we finish constructing the third water tunnel,

343
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:49,020
we'll be able to turn off the first water tunnel,

344
00:17:49,280 --> 00:17:53,400
get in there, look at what its engineering challenges are

345
00:17:53,401 --> 00:17:55,020
for something that it's 100 years old.

346
00:17:55,100 --> 00:17:57,940
People forget everything breaks at some point.

347
00:17:57,941 --> 00:18:01,040
A 100-year water tunnel at some point starts to fail,

348
00:18:01,041 --> 00:18:04,680
And you're now seeing the kind of snap, crackle, and pop

349
00:18:04,681 --> 00:18:06,980
within that system.

350
00:18:06,981 --> 00:18:09,780
Some of the Sandhawks building tunnel number three

351
00:18:09,781 --> 00:18:11,860
believe the only thing preventing tunnel number one

352
00:18:11,861 --> 00:18:14,660
from collapsing is the pressure of the water pushing

353
00:18:14,661 --> 00:18:16,120
against its walls.

354
00:18:16,121 --> 00:18:18,900
One of the engineers and contractors,

355
00:18:18,901 --> 00:18:22,060
he told me probably more starkly than anyone else,

356
00:18:22,061 --> 00:18:23,520
that what nobody knows is that we're

357
00:18:23,521 --> 00:18:25,260
facing a potential apocalypse.

358
00:18:25,261 --> 00:18:27,260
Those were his direct words.

359
00:18:27,261 --> 00:18:30,600
And so once again, an enormous project began.

360
00:18:30,601 --> 00:18:32,400
It began in 1970.

361
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:35,100
It became, for those who understood it,

362
00:18:35,200 --> 00:18:36,300
a race against the clock.

363
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,140
No one understands that race better

364
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:44,120
than the sand hogs of today, who are building city tunnel

365
00:18:44,220 --> 00:18:47,180
number three, many of whom come from a long line of tunnel

366
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:48,520
workers.

367
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:52,660
My uncle, my cousin, they are all sand hogs.

368
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:55,260
And then you join with them.

369
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:58,660
And I've been in a sand hog for 27 years.

370
00:18:58,661 --> 00:19:02,360
When I go down there the first time, it was exciting.

371
00:19:02,460 --> 00:19:04,360
I said, wow.

372
00:19:04,460 --> 00:19:08,100
Sandhog's Scotty Chessman has a PhD in geology.

373
00:19:08,200 --> 00:19:10,700
After working as a geologist for the city,

374
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:14,600
he decided to go back to the job he loved best.

375
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:16,820
You get down there, it's the root core

376
00:19:16,920 --> 00:19:19,120
of the Appalachian Mountains, anywhere from a billion years

377
00:19:19,220 --> 00:19:21,120
ago to 400 million years ago.

378
00:19:21,220 --> 00:19:23,160
If you're going through here and you see a beautiful cross

379
00:19:23,260 --> 00:19:25,920
action of what it was like with the physical activity that

380
00:19:26,020 --> 00:19:28,660
took place, the deformation, the heat and pressure,

381
00:19:28,661 --> 00:19:30,460
and the imprint on the rock.

382
00:19:30,461 --> 00:19:32,800
And it changes as you go along.

383
00:19:32,801 --> 00:19:36,000
It's almost a PhD thesis every 100 feet.

384
00:19:36,001 --> 00:19:38,500
The 60-mile-long tunnel number three

385
00:19:38,501 --> 00:19:40,800
will extend from Yonkers through the Bronx

386
00:19:40,801 --> 00:19:42,800
down to the southern tip of Manhattan

387
00:19:42,801 --> 00:19:44,900
and then over to Brooklyn and Queens.

388
00:19:44,901 --> 00:19:47,820
Longer, wider, and with more sophisticated control systems

389
00:19:47,821 --> 00:19:50,680
than its two predecessors, its construction ground

390
00:19:50,681 --> 00:19:53,520
to a halt for nearly a decade after New York City went

391
00:19:53,521 --> 00:19:56,420
bankrupt in 1974.

392
00:19:56,421 --> 00:20:00,920
and it's not scheduled for completion until 2020.

393
00:20:01,020 --> 00:20:03,520
It's being carved out by a gigantic machine,

394
00:20:03,620 --> 00:20:05,860
similar to the one that dug the channel tunnel from Britain

395
00:20:05,960 --> 00:20:10,560
to France, called a TBM, or tunnel boring machine.

396
00:20:10,660 --> 00:20:13,480
It has a large head, 13 foot roughly in diameter,

397
00:20:13,580 --> 00:20:14,980
and it has a bunch of cutters on it.

398
00:20:15,080 --> 00:20:18,080
And they press up against the face at 1.5 million pounds

399
00:20:18,180 --> 00:20:23,520
of thrust, which comes out to roughly 65,000 PSI per cutter.

400
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:25,420
This tunnel boring machine has these large grippers.

401
00:20:25,421 --> 00:20:26,920
It locks it out on the wall.

402
00:20:26,921 --> 00:20:29,220
It has these large hydraulic cylinders

403
00:20:29,221 --> 00:20:30,560
that push the head forward.

404
00:20:30,561 --> 00:20:33,160
And the head rotates and presses on the face

405
00:20:33,161 --> 00:20:36,060
and creates these chips.

406
00:20:36,061 --> 00:20:39,060
As the TBM moves forward, it's up to the Sandhogs

407
00:20:39,061 --> 00:20:41,460
to clear out the chipped rock it creates, which

408
00:20:41,461 --> 00:20:42,680
is called muck.

409
00:20:47,180 --> 00:20:48,380
Now, this is the actual muck.

410
00:20:48,381 --> 00:20:50,680
This is the last conveyor of the system, you see.

411
00:20:50,681 --> 00:20:51,880
And it's muck.

412
00:20:51,881 --> 00:20:55,420
You can see some chips in it, but it's pretty finely ground.

413
00:20:55,421 --> 00:20:58,220
So the muck you see right there probably came from the heading

414
00:20:58,221 --> 00:21:02,060
8,000 feet ahead of us probably 20 minutes ago.

415
00:21:02,061 --> 00:21:04,860
Whenever the Sandhogs prepare to descend to their workplace

416
00:21:04,861 --> 00:21:08,460
deep beneath the city, they're aware of the dangers involved,

417
00:21:08,461 --> 00:21:10,700
and that awareness keeps them alive.

418
00:21:10,701 --> 00:21:13,340
Five guys that I've worked with have been killed on the job.

419
00:21:13,341 --> 00:21:15,580
And then here, a lot of guys have been maimed.

420
00:21:15,581 --> 00:21:19,820
And it's sad that it happens, and I don't want it to happen to me.

421
00:21:19,821 --> 00:21:21,340
And they didn't want it to happen to them,

422
00:21:21,341 --> 00:21:22,780
but sometimes things happen.

423
00:21:22,781 --> 00:21:24,540
Safety's a big issue.

424
00:21:24,541 --> 00:21:26,880
Everybody wants to go home.

425
00:21:26,881 --> 00:21:28,580
There's always one last thing to do

426
00:21:28,581 --> 00:21:32,620
before getting on the elevator for the 600-foot trip down.

427
00:21:32,621 --> 00:21:34,540
This is a check-in, check-out board.

428
00:21:34,541 --> 00:21:36,460
It's very, uh, it's an OSHA requirement.

429
00:21:36,461 --> 00:21:38,160
If there's ever an accident down there,

430
00:21:38,161 --> 00:21:39,820
you'd like to know how many people are down there

431
00:21:39,821 --> 00:21:41,620
if you have to rescue anybody.

432
00:21:41,621 --> 00:21:43,460
So it's a requirement that you flip over your tag.

433
00:21:43,461 --> 00:21:46,760
So if I'm going in, I'll flip over my tag to red.

434
00:21:46,761 --> 00:21:49,360
And that indicates that I'm in the tunnel.

435
00:21:49,361 --> 00:21:51,600
24 sand hogs have died in the construction

436
00:21:51,601 --> 00:21:53,500
of tunnel number three.

437
00:21:53,501 --> 00:21:55,660
Some believe they may have given their lives

438
00:21:55,661 --> 00:21:58,060
to prevent a catastrophe.

439
00:21:58,061 --> 00:22:01,000
If one of those systems collapsed over the next 20

440
00:22:01,001 --> 00:22:06,320
years, it would be a disaster of mega proportions.

441
00:22:06,321 --> 00:22:09,740
It would overshadow all the disasters combined.

442
00:22:09,741 --> 00:22:11,940
It would truly be a calamity.

443
00:22:11,941 --> 00:22:13,960
The stakes are as high as they've ever been.

444
00:22:13,961 --> 00:22:17,200
They're as high as they were in the 1830s.

445
00:22:17,201 --> 00:22:19,580
People just don't realize it because the water is still

446
00:22:19,581 --> 00:22:20,620
flowing.

447
00:22:20,621 --> 00:22:22,520
While we're building the third water tunnel,

448
00:22:22,521 --> 00:22:28,780
We definitely and unequivocally do not expect catastrophic failure in the near term.

449
00:22:28,940 --> 00:22:32,820
But if you don't do it now and you don't start building it now,

450
00:22:32,821 --> 00:22:37,420
it's definitely not going to be a place where that risk is really facing the city.

451
00:22:38,160 --> 00:22:42,040
The city of Chicago has never had to look far for its water supply

452
00:22:42,041 --> 00:22:44,340
since it's situated on one of the Great Lakes.

453
00:22:44,680 --> 00:22:48,360
But it did have to build the largest purification plant in the world

454
00:22:48,361 --> 00:22:50,360
to keep its drinking water clean.

455
00:22:52,520 --> 00:22:56,780
New York City residents consume an average of 1.1 billion gallons of water a day.

456
00:22:57,320 --> 00:23:03,140
That's about 300 million gallons less than in 1988, when the city launched a major conservation campaign.

457
00:23:12,820 --> 00:23:21,440
Chicago, the husky, brawling city of broad shoulders, as poet Carl Sandburg once called it, is a city built on water, by water.

458
00:23:21,441 --> 00:23:26,200
Today, the Chicago water system includes more than 4,000 miles of water mains,

459
00:23:26,880 --> 00:23:31,900
ranging in size from 4 inches to 60 inches, which supply water to 5 million people.

460
00:23:33,260 --> 00:23:38,080
But in 1835, this city was home to a grand total of just 350.

461
00:23:39,180 --> 00:23:45,980
The population quickly grew to 60,000 in 15 years, and this placed a heavy burden on the city's water supply,

462
00:23:46,140 --> 00:23:51,340
which came from a water intake at the end of a 150-foot-long pier on Lake Michigan.

463
00:23:51,440 --> 00:23:54,440
It was pumped into a wooden reservoir and then flowed by

464
00:23:54,441 --> 00:23:57,440
gravity through wooden pipes into town.

465
00:23:57,441 --> 00:24:01,440
This is a wood water main, which we estimate to be over 100

466
00:24:01,441 --> 00:24:03,440
years old. It's typical of what it was

467
00:24:03,441 --> 00:24:06,440
prior to 1850. This one appears to be coated with

468
00:24:06,441 --> 00:24:09,440
some type of tar, which would probably be typical of what they

469
00:24:09,441 --> 00:24:13,440
would do when they installed it. In 1864, the city

470
00:24:13,441 --> 00:24:16,440
started construction on an engineering project that made

471
00:24:16,441 --> 00:24:19,440
headlines around the world, a tunnel that extended two miles

472
00:24:19,441 --> 00:24:22,400
that extended two miles out into Lake Michigan, the longest

473
00:24:22,401 --> 00:24:24,740
tunnel ever bored up to that time.

474
00:24:24,741 --> 00:24:28,640
The tunnel was dug through clay 60 feet below the lake's bottom

475
00:24:28,641 --> 00:24:32,680
and was lined with brick to a finished diameter of 5 feet.

476
00:24:32,681 --> 00:24:35,900
A pentagon-shaped wooden building called a crib, which

477
00:24:35,901 --> 00:24:38,900
would protect the intake shaft from ice and the elements, was

478
00:24:38,901 --> 00:24:41,900
built onshore, towed out into the lake, and sunk to the

479
00:24:41,901 --> 00:24:44,900
bottom. The term crib came from the

480
00:24:44,901 --> 00:24:47,940
wooden pilings which were sunk into the lake bed.

481
00:24:47,941 --> 00:24:49,900
30 years later, the wooden building

482
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:51,540
was replaced with a brick one.

483
00:24:51,640 --> 00:24:54,540
But the term crib remained, and the Carter Harrison Crib

484
00:24:54,640 --> 00:24:57,380
is still there today.

485
00:24:57,480 --> 00:25:00,680
The Chicago Water Department tug, which is also an icebreaker,

486
00:25:00,780 --> 00:25:04,080
makes regular trips to maintain it.

487
00:25:04,180 --> 00:25:07,160
Modern Marvels went aboard with Richard Rice, the head of the

488
00:25:07,260 --> 00:25:11,400
Chicago Water Department, for a trip to the Harrison Crib,

489
00:25:11,500 --> 00:25:15,300
which looks much as it did when it was built over 100 years ago.

490
00:25:15,301 --> 00:25:19,580
plaster walls, wood trim. We have living quarters for about

491
00:25:19,581 --> 00:25:23,480
19 individuals who may be out here at any given time during

492
00:25:23,481 --> 00:25:26,180
the year to do work. In the early days,

493
00:25:26,181 --> 00:25:28,680
there was always somebody at home at the crib.

494
00:25:28,681 --> 00:25:30,820
We actually had a crib tender.

495
00:25:30,821 --> 00:25:33,720
That was actually a title in our budget, a crib tender who

496
00:25:33,721 --> 00:25:39,160
lived here 24 hours a day. Rumor has it that they had the,

497
00:25:39,161 --> 00:25:42,200
you know, best meals in town cooked out here.

498
00:25:42,201 --> 00:25:44,820
The shore end of the tunnel was connected to a new

499
00:25:44,821 --> 00:25:47,820
pumping station that opened in 1869.

500
00:25:47,821 --> 00:25:51,360
It had the capacity to pump 18 million gallons a day, which

501
00:25:51,361 --> 00:25:56,120
was 60% more than the city needed at the time.

502
00:25:56,121 --> 00:25:57,860
The Chicago Avenue pumping station

503
00:25:57,861 --> 00:26:00,920
is still an essential part of the downtown water system,

504
00:26:00,921 --> 00:26:04,020
with a total of six working pumps that can produce 200

505
00:26:04,021 --> 00:26:06,520
million gallons a day.

506
00:26:06,521 --> 00:26:09,180
The original pumps were steam driven.

507
00:26:09,181 --> 00:26:11,680
We've converted and modernized the station.

508
00:26:11,681 --> 00:26:14,480
These are one of our older electric pumps.

509
00:26:14,481 --> 00:26:18,480
here will be pumping 30 million gallons per day.

510
00:26:18,481 --> 00:26:21,480
The Chicago Avenue pumping station, which now includes a

511
00:26:21,481 --> 00:26:24,480
restaurant and a theater, and the historic water tower across

512
00:26:24,481 --> 00:26:27,480
the street, have become major tourist attractions in downtown

513
00:26:27,481 --> 00:26:32,480
Chicago. For a while, the two-mile long

514
00:26:32,481 --> 00:26:36,480
water tunnel under Lake Michigan provided an abundant supply of

515
00:26:36,481 --> 00:26:41,480
clean, fresh water for Chicago. But as the city grew, tanneries,

516
00:26:41,481 --> 00:26:44,780
factories, and other new industries belched effluent

517
00:26:44,781 --> 00:26:47,280
directly into the Chicago River, which was already

518
00:26:47,281 --> 00:26:51,280
polluted with sewage. By 1890, Chicago's sewage was

519
00:26:51,281 --> 00:26:54,140
flowing all the way from the Chicago River to the intake

520
00:26:54,141 --> 00:26:57,980
crib two miles offshore. Chicago was hit by outbreaks

521
00:26:57,981 --> 00:27:01,580
of dysentery and typhoid. To keep pollution out of the

522
00:27:01,581 --> 00:27:06,040
lake, the city broke ground in 1892 on the largest earth-moving

523
00:27:06,041 --> 00:27:09,980
project of its time, the 28-mile-long sanitary and

524
00:27:09,981 --> 00:27:14,980
the Suez Canal in Egypt. The canal reversed the flow of the

525
00:27:14,981 --> 00:27:17,980
Chicago River, making it the first river in history to flow

526
00:27:17,981 --> 00:27:22,980
away from its mouth. 8,500 laborers used mule-drawn

527
00:27:22,981 --> 00:27:26,980
plows, steam shovels, and dynamite to move nearly 30

528
00:27:26,981 --> 00:27:30,980
million cubic yards of earth. Reversing the river's flow led

529
00:27:30,981 --> 00:27:33,980
to a dramatic reduction of pollution in Lake Michigan, and

530
00:27:33,981 --> 00:27:38,980
by 1908, Chicago's typhoid rate had dropped 91%.

531
00:27:38,981 --> 00:27:43,160
Chicago eventually built more intakes in cribs to bring water

532
00:27:43,161 --> 00:27:45,780
from Lake Michigan. They include the still

533
00:27:45,781 --> 00:27:49,220
functioning Deaver Crib, located beside the old Harrison Crib,

534
00:27:49,221 --> 00:27:52,660
and connected to it by a steel walkway.

535
00:27:52,661 --> 00:27:58,660
In 1935, the Deaver Crib was completed, and that was actually

536
00:27:58,661 --> 00:28:04,500
a 16-foot concrete-lined pipe that actually is beneath the

537
00:28:04,501 --> 00:28:09,700
the floor of Lake Michigan at a depth of about 150 feet.

538
00:28:09,701 --> 00:28:13,200
The Harrison Crib, taken out of service in 1998,

539
00:28:13,201 --> 00:28:15,840
still serves as the living quarters for maintenance crews

540
00:28:15,841 --> 00:28:20,660
working on the Deaver Crib. As Chicago's population grew,

541
00:28:20,661 --> 00:28:23,860
a large purification plant became necessary.

542
00:28:23,861 --> 00:28:27,060
The Jardine Purification Plant, designed to filter and treat

543
00:28:27,061 --> 00:28:29,500
more than a million gallons of water per minute,

544
00:28:29,501 --> 00:28:33,000
was completed in 1964. It's the largest water treatment

545
00:28:33,001 --> 00:28:37,500
plant in the world. Huge pumps bring lake water

546
00:28:37,501 --> 00:28:40,440
from the Deaver crib into the plant, where it goes through a

547
00:28:40,441 --> 00:28:44,240
vast system of sand and gravel filters to remove impurities,

548
00:28:44,241 --> 00:28:47,540
called particulate. Each one of these filters is

549
00:28:47,541 --> 00:28:51,160
a conventional rapid sand filter. It's basically a couple feet of

550
00:28:51,161 --> 00:28:55,060
gravel topped by a couple feet of sand, and the water travels

551
00:28:55,061 --> 00:28:58,700
through the sand and is filtered. It will trap most of the

552
00:28:58,701 --> 00:29:02,260
particulate on the top. Aluminum sulfate is added

553
00:29:02,261 --> 00:29:05,740
to cause particulate to coagulate into larger particles called

554
00:29:05,741 --> 00:29:07,740
floc.

555
00:29:07,741 --> 00:29:10,040
This happens in flocculation basins,

556
00:29:10,041 --> 00:29:12,840
where a large paddle wheel stirs the mixture.

557
00:29:12,841 --> 00:29:16,200
Each one of these flocculation basin has four passes where we

558
00:29:16,201 --> 00:29:20,740
slow stir the water to form the floc before it starts its journey

559
00:29:20,741 --> 00:29:23,140
through the settling process.

560
00:29:23,141 --> 00:29:25,480
Several other chemicals are added to the water before it

561
00:29:25,481 --> 00:29:27,480
leaves the Jardine plant.

562
00:29:27,481 --> 00:29:30,460
They include chlorine for disinfection, fluoride to fight

563
00:29:30,461 --> 00:29:33,760
tooth decay, and blended phosphates to prevent corrosion

564
00:29:33,761 --> 00:29:34,760
in water mains and pipes.

565
00:29:38,620 --> 00:29:40,600
In the plant's testing laboratory,

566
00:29:40,601 --> 00:29:42,640
technicians perform numerous tests

567
00:29:42,641 --> 00:29:45,700
to monitor the quality of the water 24 hours a day,

568
00:29:45,701 --> 00:29:48,700
365 days a year.

569
00:29:48,701 --> 00:29:51,400
Millions of samples are taken at the plant every year

570
00:29:51,401 --> 00:29:53,640
to ensure that the water meets the standards of the Safe

571
00:29:53,641 --> 00:29:56,140
Drinking Water Act.

572
00:29:56,141 --> 00:29:58,100
The incoming raw water is also monitored

573
00:29:58,101 --> 00:30:01,700
unpleasant tastes or odors. With the odor monitors, we

574
00:30:01,701 --> 00:30:06,140
basically use the human nose. In raw water, you might smell an

575
00:30:06,141 --> 00:30:10,540
earthy smell, you may smell a fishy smell, you may smell a

576
00:30:10,541 --> 00:30:14,860
moldy smell, you may smell a chemical or a phenol smell.

577
00:30:14,861 --> 00:30:18,260
If the water fails the sniff test, the Jardine plant will

578
00:30:18,261 --> 00:30:21,500
add powdered activated carbon, which can remove tastes or

579
00:30:21,501 --> 00:30:24,160
odors. Tiny carbon particles are extremely

580
00:30:24,161 --> 00:30:28,000
absorbent, like sponges. After the water

581
00:30:28,001 --> 00:30:30,300
has completed the treatment process,

582
00:30:30,400 --> 00:30:34,400
it flows by gravity through our outlet tunnels

583
00:30:34,500 --> 00:30:38,240
to our 12 pumping stations located throughout the city.

584
00:30:38,340 --> 00:30:40,240
Chicago's location on Lake Michigan

585
00:30:40,340 --> 00:30:43,740
means it has a huge supply of fresh water on its doorstep.

586
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:46,360
Los Angeles has no such luck.

587
00:30:46,460 --> 00:30:48,660
So finding the water necessary to turn this patch

588
00:30:48,760 --> 00:30:52,300
of semi-arid land into America's second largest city

589
00:30:52,400 --> 00:30:55,760
has never been easy.

590
00:30:55,761 --> 00:30:58,660
The Chicago Avenue pumping station and water tower were

591
00:30:58,760 --> 00:31:01,560
the only public buildings to survive in the area destroyed

592
00:31:01,660 --> 00:31:03,660
by the Chicago Fire of 1871.

593
00:31:12,120 --> 00:31:14,120
The Diamond Valley Lake Reservoir,

594
00:31:14,220 --> 00:31:16,920
about 80 miles east of Los Angeles.

595
00:31:17,020 --> 00:31:19,760
It was the largest earthworks construction project in US

596
00:31:19,860 --> 00:31:23,460
history and one of the boldest ideas for water storage

597
00:31:23,560 --> 00:31:25,760
ever conceived.

598
00:31:25,761 --> 00:31:29,760
The Metropolitan Water District and the engineers there looked

599
00:31:29,860 --> 00:31:34,260
at what was essentially a ring of mountains and said,

600
00:31:34,360 --> 00:31:36,700
what if we connected them?

601
00:31:36,800 --> 00:31:41,400
And so they created a bowl that is elevated to the mountaintops.

602
00:31:41,500 --> 00:31:44,020
It's an extraordinary idea.

603
00:31:44,120 --> 00:31:48,720
Unlike most dams, a river does not run through it.

604
00:31:48,820 --> 00:31:51,460
Water for the huge reservoir comes by pipeline from the

605
00:31:51,560 --> 00:31:54,260
state water system in California's Central Valley

606
00:31:54,261 --> 00:31:58,160
and from the Colorado River. The Diamond Valley Lake Reservoir

607
00:31:58,161 --> 00:32:01,260
has doubled the water storage capacity in Southern California.

608
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:10,360
Construction began in 1995, and the reservoir was completed five

609
00:32:10,361 --> 00:32:13,800
years later. $100 million worth of earth-moving

610
00:32:13,801 --> 00:32:17,360
equipment, including some of the largest dump trucks in the world,

611
00:32:17,361 --> 00:32:21,760
was used to carve out nearly 150 million cubic yards of soil

612
00:32:21,761 --> 00:32:24,260
and rock. You put that on a scale of

613
00:32:24,261 --> 00:32:26,760
So those kind of projects have been done worldwide.

614
00:32:26,761 --> 00:32:30,500
That's seventh largest in the world in terms of moving that

615
00:32:30,501 --> 00:32:34,800
amount of material, but we did it quite fast.

616
00:32:34,801 --> 00:32:38,000
And we were moving enormous volumes of earth and rock every

617
00:32:38,001 --> 00:32:44,080
day, 20 hours a day, six days a week for four years to do that.

618
00:32:44,081 --> 00:32:46,940
Once the dams were completed, the next step was to fill the

619
00:32:46,941 --> 00:32:49,180
reservoir with water.

620
00:32:49,181 --> 00:32:52,420
With a capacity of over a quarter of a trillion gallons, it took

621
00:32:52,421 --> 00:32:55,720
than two years to fill it up. The Diamond Valley Lake project

622
00:32:55,721 --> 00:32:58,220
includes a large pumping station to get the water into the

623
00:32:58,221 --> 00:33:01,920
reservoir. The pumps actually draw off that

624
00:33:01,921 --> 00:33:06,060
smaller lake or that pumping pool and push water through the

625
00:33:06,061 --> 00:33:09,980
mountain that we're standing on to an outlet tower which allows

626
00:33:09,981 --> 00:33:14,920
the water to be delivered into the lake. The pumping station

627
00:33:14,921 --> 00:33:17,680
contains some of the largest variable speed electric motors

628
00:33:17,681 --> 00:33:21,320
that have ever been built. The variable speeds allow them to

629
00:33:21,321 --> 00:33:24,760
to changing water levels in the reservoir.

630
00:33:24,761 --> 00:33:27,860
Motors can also function as generators when the flow of water

631
00:33:27,861 --> 00:33:32,260
through the pumps is reversed. Each of the 12 motors is a

632
00:33:32,261 --> 00:33:35,560
Westinghouse 6,000 horsepower motor. They were built in Texas

633
00:33:35,561 --> 00:33:38,280
and they were transported here and then placed on top of the

634
00:33:38,281 --> 00:33:41,280
Mitsubishi pumps. They're capable of pumping 175

635
00:33:41,281 --> 00:33:43,980
cubic feet per second. Now the unique thing about these

636
00:33:43,981 --> 00:33:46,380
particular units is they're also capable of running in a

637
00:33:46,381 --> 00:33:49,880
generator mode, in which case we can produce 3.3 megawatts of

638
00:33:49,881 --> 00:33:53,880
electricity when they're in gen mode.

639
00:33:53,881 --> 00:33:57,880
The Diamond Valley Lake project is a visionary investment in the future.

640
00:33:57,881 --> 00:34:01,880
As with projects launched by California water planners decades ago,

641
00:34:01,881 --> 00:34:05,880
it's expected to pay huge dividends.

642
00:34:05,881 --> 00:34:09,880
If ever there was a city built by water, it's Los Angeles.

643
00:34:09,881 --> 00:34:16,880
We've always used water in California as a means of removing the natural limits.

644
00:34:16,881 --> 00:34:21,320
Clearly, God never intended large numbers of people to live in the Los Angeles basin.

645
00:34:21,520 --> 00:34:22,920
It's a semi-airic plain.

646
00:34:23,700 --> 00:34:28,880
And yet we had the accidental truth about the development of California is that everyone

647
00:34:28,881 --> 00:34:35,340
came west initially for the gold rush and then turned left, going south to the area

648
00:34:35,341 --> 00:34:36,580
where the water was not.

649
00:34:37,500 --> 00:34:42,160
The story of the early growth of the L.A. water system is largely the story of one

650
00:34:42,161 --> 00:34:46,160
the first director of the city's water department.

651
00:34:46,161 --> 00:34:50,160
He was an immigrant from Ireland who first worked as a ditch

652
00:34:50,161 --> 00:34:54,160
tender for the city. It's very rough, uneducated,

653
00:34:54,161 --> 00:34:58,160
not trained as an engineer, but it's supremely confident.

654
00:34:58,161 --> 00:35:02,160
By the age of 31, Mulholland was head of the Los Angeles

655
00:35:02,161 --> 00:35:05,160
Water Department and searching desperately for new sources of

656
00:35:05,161 --> 00:35:07,160
water for the city's growing population.

657
00:35:07,161 --> 00:35:11,160
That water would come from the Owens River, more than 200 miles

658
00:35:11,161 --> 00:35:15,800
north. Construction on the 223-mile-long

659
00:35:15,801 --> 00:35:20,440
Los Angeles aqueduct began in 1908 after a newspaper campaign

660
00:35:20,441 --> 00:35:23,940
to get Los Angeles residents to support the idea of a public bond

661
00:35:23,941 --> 00:35:29,020
issue to pay for it. Simultaneously, the city fathers

662
00:35:29,021 --> 00:35:32,580
announced the existence of a drought, a drought that no one had

663
00:35:32,581 --> 00:35:36,260
noticed before. But in order to float the bonds

664
00:35:36,261 --> 00:35:40,120
that were necessary to secure voter approval, it wasn't enough

665
00:35:40,121 --> 00:35:42,920
simply to say that there was an abundant supply available for

666
00:35:42,921 --> 00:35:47,560
future needs. What followed then was a largely fraudulent, as it

667
00:35:47,561 --> 00:35:52,440
turns out, campaign, but only the first of many by which the fear

668
00:35:52,441 --> 00:35:56,840
of an impending drought was used to create popular support for new

669
00:35:56,841 --> 00:36:00,440
waterworks. With the financing in place,

670
00:36:00,441 --> 00:36:04,680
construction finally began on 34 miles of open, unlined channel,

671
00:36:04,681 --> 00:36:08,980
miles of concrete channel, and 98 miles of covered concrete

672
00:36:08,981 --> 00:36:11,980
conduit. Some of the conduit was big enough

673
00:36:11,981 --> 00:36:17,020
to drive a car through. Six million pounds of blasting

674
00:36:17,021 --> 00:36:19,920
powder were used to build the aqueduct.

675
00:36:19,921 --> 00:36:23,540
When it finally went into service in 1913, bottles of

676
00:36:23,541 --> 00:36:27,040
Owens River water were handed out to the 30,000 celebrants,

677
00:36:27,041 --> 00:36:31,040
who traveled by car, wagon, and buggy to the opening ceremony

678
00:36:31,041 --> 00:36:34,100
ceremony at the San Fernando Reservoir, north of the city

679
00:36:34,101 --> 00:36:37,000
center. Although residents of Los Angeles

680
00:36:37,001 --> 00:36:40,180
welcomed the new water supply, farmers in the Owens Valley

681
00:36:40,181 --> 00:36:42,880
weren't too happy about having millions of gallons of their

682
00:36:42,881 --> 00:36:48,440
water siphoned off daily. In 1923, both the city and the

683
00:36:48,441 --> 00:36:52,000
Owens Valley area were experiencing water shortages.

684
00:36:52,001 --> 00:36:54,800
Confrontations escalated, and there were incidents of

685
00:36:54,801 --> 00:36:58,940
sabotage. During the 1920s, there were 10 attempts to blow

686
00:36:58,941 --> 00:37:00,915
up the aqueduct with dynamite.

687
00:37:00,916 --> 00:37:05,260
The construction supervisor at Diamond Valley Lake, Dennis Majors,

688
00:37:05,261 --> 00:37:10,380
recalls his grandfather's stories of patrolling the aqueduct on horseback with a rifle to prevent

689
00:37:10,381 --> 00:37:11,081
sabotage.

690
00:37:11,082 --> 00:37:14,860
They had to get out there every day early in the morning starting at 5 o'clock and

691
00:37:14,861 --> 00:37:17,972
head 25 miles in one direction patrolling that thing.

692
00:37:17,973 --> 00:37:20,380
They did tell me, well, they never had to

693
00:37:20,381 --> 00:37:25,183
arrest anybody, but they probably got close from time to time.

694
00:37:25,184 --> 00:37:27,740
He carried an old 1870 Winchester

695
00:37:27,741 --> 00:37:32,280
2535 with a scabbard on his horse.

696
00:37:32,281 --> 00:37:37,380
And maybe that's had some effect on not having to arrest anyone.

697
00:37:37,381 --> 00:37:39,560
The Los Angeles Department of Water and Power

698
00:37:39,561 --> 00:37:41,820
that Mulholland helped create went on

699
00:37:41,821 --> 00:37:45,360
to become the largest municipal utility in the country.

700
00:37:45,361 --> 00:37:47,660
But Mulholland's career ended unhappily,

701
00:37:47,661 --> 00:37:51,400
with his resignation in 1928 after the collapse of the St.

702
00:37:51,401 --> 00:37:54,560
Francis Dam near Los Angeles.

703
00:37:54,561 --> 00:37:57,260
Mulholland had supervised the dam's construction

704
00:37:57,261 --> 00:38:01,660
and inspected it just hours before it burst, killing 450

705
00:38:01,661 --> 00:38:06,060
people. Because of the city's huge population

706
00:38:06,061 --> 00:38:10,200
growth, Los Angeles now relies on a complex system that delivers

707
00:38:10,201 --> 00:38:13,240
water not only from the Owens Valley, but also from the

708
00:38:13,241 --> 00:38:16,580
Sacramento and San Joaquin Rivers via the California

709
00:38:16,581 --> 00:38:20,480
Aqueduct, and the Colorado River via the Colorado River

710
00:38:20,481 --> 00:38:24,480
Aqueduct. The water sources are

711
00:38:24,481 --> 00:38:26,960
coordinated by the Metropolitan Water District of Southern

712
00:38:26,961 --> 00:38:29,960
California. Formed by Los Angeles and surrounding

713
00:38:29,961 --> 00:38:34,960
municipalities in 1928, the MWD now encompasses an area from

714
00:38:34,961 --> 00:38:37,960
Ventura in the north to San Diego in the south.

715
00:38:37,961 --> 00:38:42,960
Not just Los Angeles, but its child, the Metropolitan Water

716
00:38:42,961 --> 00:38:47,960
District, have always built way ahead of demand, have more

717
00:38:47,961 --> 00:38:50,960
water than they are likely ever to need and have been able to

718
00:38:50,961 --> 00:38:53,960
survive the droughts that have struck California in the last

719
00:38:53,961 --> 00:38:58,960
in far better condition than many other parts of the state.

720
00:38:58,961 --> 00:39:02,960
One huge untapped source of drinking water for California

721
00:39:02,961 --> 00:39:05,960
is the Pacific Ocean. Desalination plants can change

722
00:39:05,961 --> 00:39:09,960
seawater into freshwater, but the only plant doing it on a

723
00:39:09,961 --> 00:39:14,960
large scale is 3,000 miles away from Los Angeles.

724
00:39:14,961 --> 00:39:18,960
The Metropolitan Water District of Southern California provides

725
00:39:18,961 --> 00:39:22,960
residents with up to 800 billion gallons of water every year.

726
00:39:22,961 --> 00:39:36,100
The Tampa Bay Seawater Desalination Plant is the only

727
00:39:36,101 --> 00:39:39,920
operating seawater plant of its kind in the country.

728
00:39:39,921 --> 00:39:43,020
The plant serves about 2.5 million Florida residents in

729
00:39:43,021 --> 00:39:47,060
Tampa, St. Petersburg, and the surrounding area, providing 10

730
00:39:47,061 --> 00:39:50,260
percent of the fresh water supply, or about 25 million

731
00:39:50,261 --> 00:39:52,520
gallons a day.

732
00:39:52,521 --> 00:39:54,680
To change seawater into drinking water,

733
00:39:54,681 --> 00:39:59,860
the plant uses a process known as reverse osmosis, or RO.

734
00:39:59,861 --> 00:40:01,820
This is raw seawater.

735
00:40:01,821 --> 00:40:04,620
This is the water before it goes into the first stage

736
00:40:04,621 --> 00:40:08,180
of the filtration system, the sand filters.

737
00:40:08,181 --> 00:40:11,240
The sand filters are the next stage of filtration

738
00:40:11,241 --> 00:40:14,800
before the water gets into the RO process.

739
00:40:14,801 --> 00:40:16,320
The water is pumped through filters

740
00:40:16,321 --> 00:40:19,140
to remove impurities before entering the reverse osmosis

741
00:40:19,141 --> 00:40:21,260
system.

742
00:40:21,261 --> 00:40:25,980
These are five micron cartridge filters that are made of like a yarn substance.

743
00:40:26,680 --> 00:40:32,400
These filters are able to capture any suspended solids that may make their way through the sand filters.

744
00:40:32,401 --> 00:40:38,900
We don't want any particles, any suspended particles, passing on beyond this point in the process

745
00:40:38,901 --> 00:40:42,460
because that could result in fouling of the reverse osmosis membranes.

746
00:40:43,300 --> 00:40:48,320
In regular osmosis, when two water volumes are separated by a semi-permeable membrane,

747
00:40:48,321 --> 00:40:53,680
water will flow from the side of lowest concentration of soluble matter, like pure water,

748
00:40:54,040 --> 00:40:57,880
to the side of highest concentration of soluble matter, like salt water.

749
00:40:59,620 --> 00:41:04,400
In reverse osmosis, pressure is exerted on the side with a concentrated solution,

750
00:41:04,740 --> 00:41:09,280
the seawater, to force water molecules across the membrane to the freshwater side,

751
00:41:10,000 --> 00:41:14,820
in effect, filtering out the salt. Following the cartridge filtration step,

752
00:41:14,821 --> 00:41:20,360
the saltwater pressure is then boosted with this 2,000 horsepower high-pressure pump.

753
00:41:20,780 --> 00:41:25,640
That pump pressurizes the water up to 1,000 pounds per square inch.

754
00:41:26,040 --> 00:41:29,560
That's enough pressure then to pass on to the membranes,

755
00:41:29,780 --> 00:41:32,920
which is really the heart of the reverse osmosis process.

756
00:41:34,560 --> 00:41:39,180
Each one of the blue pressure vessels contains eight of these reverse osmosis tubes.

757
00:41:39,920 --> 00:41:43,720
This is a cross-section of a reverse osmosis membrane.

758
00:41:43,721 --> 00:41:47,320
The way this works, the salt water under the high pressure,

759
00:41:47,420 --> 00:41:50,420
1,000 pounds per square inch, is passed into the membrane.

760
00:41:50,520 --> 00:41:54,860
That high pressure forces the fresh water through the membrane,

761
00:41:54,960 --> 00:41:58,760
leaving behind a salty, concentrated solution.

762
00:41:58,860 --> 00:42:01,180
In the entire plant, there are 10,000

763
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:03,380
reverse osmosis membranes.

764
00:42:03,480 --> 00:42:05,680
If you unrolled all these membranes

765
00:42:05,780 --> 00:42:08,980
and just put the membrane surface area back to back,

766
00:42:09,080 --> 00:42:12,620
it would stretch for 223 miles.

767
00:42:12,621 --> 00:42:16,020
The concentrated seawater left over from the desalination

768
00:42:16,120 --> 00:42:20,520
process is diluted and released back into the ocean.

769
00:42:20,620 --> 00:42:23,660
The desalination project at Tampa Bay is being studied

770
00:42:23,760 --> 00:42:26,660
closely by other water departments across the U.S.,

771
00:42:26,760 --> 00:42:28,760
including the Metropolitan Water District of Southern

772
00:42:28,860 --> 00:42:32,580
California. The question will be when we go

773
00:42:32,680 --> 00:42:36,580
there, not whether we go there, in my opinion.

774
00:42:36,680 --> 00:42:40,580
A seawater desalination program in Southern California could be

775
00:42:40,581 --> 00:42:44,760
a very important complement to the other water resources,

776
00:42:44,761 --> 00:42:47,580
so that when we have droughts, or if we had an earthquake

777
00:42:47,581 --> 00:42:50,160
that knocked out one of our pipelines from the Colorado

778
00:42:50,161 --> 00:42:54,020
River or the State Water Project, you have a pallet

779
00:42:54,021 --> 00:42:55,920
of resources that you can draw upon,

780
00:42:55,921 --> 00:43:00,840
and seawater desalination would be a nice complement to that.

781
00:43:00,841 --> 00:43:03,240
As fresh, pure water becomes an increasingly

782
00:43:03,241 --> 00:43:07,400
valuable resource, some experts are calling it the new oil

783
00:43:07,401 --> 00:43:11,000
and predicting that wars may someday be fought over it.

784
00:43:11,001 --> 00:43:13,940
Ideas that would have been considered bizarre a few years

785
00:43:13,941 --> 00:43:16,940
ago are looking increasingly viable.

786
00:43:16,941 --> 00:43:20,340
A Greek company, Aquarius Water Transportation,

787
00:43:20,341 --> 00:43:23,640
has been supplying fresh water to several Greek islands using

788
00:43:23,641 --> 00:43:27,360
huge floating plastic bags. The bags, which are towed to

789
00:43:27,361 --> 00:43:30,760
the destination by tugboat, can each hold nearly 8 million

790
00:43:30,761 --> 00:43:34,300
gallons of water. Another company has proposed

791
00:43:34,301 --> 00:43:37,800
has proposed wrapping entire icebergs in plastic and towing

792
00:43:37,801 --> 00:43:40,400
them to locations that need water.

793
00:43:40,401 --> 00:43:43,900
70% of the Earth's fresh water is locked up in the polar ice

794
00:43:43,901 --> 00:43:46,400
gaps from which icebergs are formed.

795
00:43:46,401 --> 00:43:49,600
People have been talking about icebergs for a long time.

796
00:43:49,601 --> 00:43:52,800
It's sort of a captivating idea.

797
00:43:52,801 --> 00:43:57,000
And there are certainly areas of the world that

798
00:43:57,001 --> 00:44:00,040
have limited options.

799
00:44:00,041 --> 00:44:03,900
And just towing down an iceberg to areas of the Middle

800
00:44:03,901 --> 00:44:08,640
or even some islands in the South Pacific that are limited in

801
00:44:08,641 --> 00:44:12,840
their water supplies could be very attractive.

802
00:44:12,841 --> 00:44:15,640
Futuristic techniques like these may be practical in

803
00:44:15,641 --> 00:44:19,340
isolated applications, but for the most part, public water

804
00:44:19,341 --> 00:44:22,660
supplies seem destined for the foreseeable future to continue

805
00:44:22,661 --> 00:44:25,660
using tried and true methods, some of which date back

806
00:44:25,661 --> 00:44:29,060
thousands of years. It's hard to beat rivers,

807
00:44:29,061 --> 00:44:32,960
reservoirs, pipes, and pumps, but unless today's water planners

808
00:44:32,961 --> 00:44:36,460
vision, foresight, and public support that their predecessors

809
00:44:36,461 --> 00:44:42,460
had decades ago, the consequences could be dire.

